body:
I downloaded the texture that @thundershield provided and extracted all mipmaps out of it using nvidia tool:
```
nvdecompress -mipmaps -format png ../dds_120_with_mipmaps.dds
```

And got ethalon mipmap resolutions:
```
>file *.png
dds_120_with_mipmaps_mipmap0.png: PNG image data, 120 x 120, 8-bit/color RGBA, non-interlaced
dds_120_with_mipmaps_mipmap1.png: PNG image data, 60 x 60, 8-bit/color RGBA, non-interlaced
dds_120_with_mipmaps_mipmap2.png: PNG image data, 30 x 30, 8-bit/color RGBA, non-interlaced
dds_120_with_mipmaps_mipmap3.png: PNG image data, 15 x 15, 8-bit/color RGBA, non-interlaced
dds_120_with_mipmaps_mipmap4.png: PNG image data, 7 x 7, 8-bit/color RGBA, non-interlaced
dds_120_with_mipmaps_mipmap5.png: PNG image data, 3 x 3, 8-bit/color RGBA, non-interlaced
dds_120_with_mipmaps_mipmap6.png: PNG image data, 1 x 1, 8-bit/color RGBA, non-interlaced
```

I also extracted some code from godot library to be able to run it standalone. As you can see below, two different branches of code in Godot produce different mipmap sizes:
```
void mipmap_uncompressed() {
    std::cout<<"Mipmap calculator (uncompressed)\n";

    int p_mipmaps = 7;

    int w = 120;
    int h = 120;

    printf("mipmap=%d width=%d height=%d\n", 0, w, h);
	for (int i = 1; i < p_mipmaps; i++) {
		w = (w + 1) >> 1;
		h = (h + 1) >> 1;

		printf("mipmap=%d width=%d height=%d\n", i, w, h);
	}
}


Mipmap calculator (uncompressed)
mipmap=0 width=120 height=120
mipmap=1 width=60 height=60
mipmap=2 width=30 height=30
mipmap=3 width=15 height=15
mipmap=4 width=8 height=8
mipmap=5 width=4 height=4
mipmap=6 width=2 height=2

void mipmap_compressed() {
    std::cout<<"Mipmap calculator (compressed)\n";

    int p_mipmaps = 7;

    int w = 120;
    int h = 120;

    printf("mipmap=%d width=%d height=%d\n", 0, w, h);
	for (int i = 1; i < p_mipmaps; i++) {
		w = MAX(1u, w >> 1);
		h = MAX(1u, h >> 1);

		printf("mipmap=%d width=%d height=%d\n", i, w, h);
	}
}


Mipmap calculator (compressed)
mipmap=0 width=120 height=120
mipmap=1 width=60 height=60
mipmap=2 width=30 height=30
mipmap=3 width=15 height=15
mipmap=4 width=7 height=7
mipmap=5 width=3 height=3
mipmap=6 width=1 height=1
```

And if you [look into the DDSTextureLoader code by Microsoft](https://github.com/microsoft/DirectXTex/blob/main/DDSTextureLoader/DDSTextureLoader9.cpp#L894) (MIT license btw), it indeed uses MAX(1u, w >> 1) approach, so the `uncomressed` branch in Godot is incorrect.


I opened pull request for this: https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/105193

